  "identity": {
    "moved_rule_text": "---\ncore: false\nload: always_on\ninstall: hook-deliver\n---\n## Git Conventions\n\nSee also: [`pr-conventions.md`](pr-conventions.md) for PR workflow, merge policy, and the `.ai-state/` safety contract at PR time (path-scoped; loads when editing PR-adjacent surfaces).\n\n### Commit Scope\n\n- One logical change per commit \u2014 avoid mixing unrelated changes\n- Prefer small, focused commits over large sweeping ones\n- Separate refactoring from behavior changes into distinct commits\n- Batch `.ai-state/` bookkeeping into one `chore(state)` commit per boundary (merge, push, session end); hook leftovers (finalize outputs, ledger moves, summary tail) ride the next state commit, never their own\n- ML experiment branches (`experiment/<run-tag>`, `exp/<run-tag>`) use extended commit semantics \u2014 a kept training run is the commit unit. Path-scoped in [`ml/experiment-commit-conventions.md`](../../ml/experiment-commit-conventions.md), loaded on training files.\n\n### Staging Discipline\n\n- Stage specific files by name \u2014 avoid `git add -A` or `git add .`\n- Review `git diff --staged` before every commit\n- Verify the staged diff matches intent \u2014 no accidental inclusions\n- **Never a bare `git stash` / `git stash pop` in a worktree concurrent agents may share** \u2014 it sweeps up every other writer's uncommitted work regardless of pathspec staging discipline. Prefer a temporary WIP commit to set work aside. If a stash is unavoidable: `git stash push -u -m \"<unique-tag>\"`, capture the SHA immediately via `git stash list --format='%H %gs'`, restore with `git stash apply <sha>` (never `pop`), and drop the entry afterward by re-finding it by tag.\n- **Treat any git command that is routine on a clean tree as destructive on a dirty one.** Same class as the stash rule above, and it has cost real work here more than once. `git checkout -- <path>` discards uncommitted changes silently \u2014 never reach for it to undo a formatter; save the bytes and restore them, asserting a hash match. **`pre-commit`'s own stash/restore is not a no-op when a hook rewrites a file**: it stashes unstaged changes, runs hooks, then restores *over* whatever the hooks wrote. With a formatter version mismatch this never converges \u2014 each attempt reformats and the restore reverts it, `git add` never captures a stable state, and completed work can vanish while `git status` reports the directory clean. Recovery, if it happens: the stashes persist as `~/.cache/pre-commit/patch*`; find the newest one containing your content and re-apply it selectively with `git apply --include=<path>`. Prevention: keep the pinned and installed formatter versions equal (`scripts/check_ruff_pin_drift.py` enforces this for ruff).\n- **Verify what a commit *contains*, not that it succeeded.** A commit can report success having written only some of what was staged. After any commit that matters, check the committed tree \u2014 `git show HEAD:<path> | grep <marker>` \u2014 rather than trusting the exit code.\n\n### Secrets and Sensitive Files\n\n- Never commit secrets, credentials, API keys, or tokens\n- Never commit `.env`, `credentials.json`, or similar config with secrets\n- If a secret is accidentally staged, unstage it immediately \u2014 do not proceed with the commit\n- When in doubt about whether a file contains sensitive data, err on the side of exclusion\n\n### What Not to Commit\n\n- Generated files (build artifacts, compiled output, lock files not meant to be tracked)\n- Large binaries or media files unless the project explicitly tracks them\n- Debug print statements or temporary logging left from investigation\n- Commented-out code blocks \u2014 delete dead code, don't preserve it in comments\n\n### Commit Message Format\n\n```\n<type>: <subject>\n\n<body>\n```\n\n**Subject line:**\n\n- Use imperative mood (\"Add\" not \"Added\", \"Fix\" not \"Fixed\")\n- Keep under 50 characters\n- No period at the end\n- Be specific: \"Fix null check in auth handler\" not \"Fix bug\"\n\n**Type prefix** (optional but recommended):\n\n- `feat`: new feature\n- `fix`: bug fix\n- `refactor`: code restructuring without behavior change\n- `docs`: documentation only\n- `test`: adding/updating tests\n- `chore`: maintenance, dependencies, config\n\n**Body** (when changes need context):\n\n- Separate from subject with blank line\n- Wrap at 72 characters\n- Explain *what* and *why*, not *how*\n- Reference related issues if applicable\n- Never include AI authorship lines (`Co-Authored-By`, `Generated by`, etc.) \u2014 the human owns the commit\n\n### Examples\n\nSimple change:\n\n```\nfix: Handle empty input in validation\n```\n\nChange needing context:\n\n```\nfeat: Add retry logic for API calls\n\nTransient network failures were causing silent data loss.\nRetry up to 3 times with exponential backoff before failing.\n```\n",
